---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Gas Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-gas-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Heat Pump Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-heat-pump-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Pipe Insulation",
    "url": "/best-pipe-insulation/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Electric Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-electric-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best 50 Gallon Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-50-gallon-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "How Long Do Water Heaters Last?",
    "url": "/how-long-do-water-heaters-last/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-25", modified: "2026-11-25" });
const title = "7 Best Water Heater Blankets in 2026: R-Value, Fit and Safe Clearances";
const description =
  "The best water heater blankets and jackets from Frost King, SmartJacket, AES and Trade Winds, compared on R-value, tank fit and fiberglass vs foil, with the gas and electric clearance rules.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "frost-king-sp57-11c",
    "name": "Frost King SP57/11C All Season Water Heater Insulation Blanket",
    "category": "Vinyl-Backed Fiberglass, R10, 3 in Thick, 48 x 75 in, Tanks up to 60 Gal",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "R10 rating, the highest of the fiberglass blankets here, per the listing",
      "3 inches of fiberglass gives real insulation rather than a reflective claim",
      "Vinyl backing keeps the fiberglass contained and wipes clean",
      "Rated for gas, oil and electric water heaters up to 60 gallons",
      "Roll of plastic tape included for seams and cut-outs"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Fiberglass needs gloves, long sleeves and a mask to cut",
      "Bulky: the 3-inch thickness makes it harder to fit in a tight closet",
      "48 by 75 inches may not wrap a wide 60-gallon tank with a full overlap"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best water heater blanket for most older 30 to 50 gallon tanks: a genuine R10 fiberglass wrap from a long-standing weatherization brand.",
    "description": "If your tank is warm to the touch and you want the most insulation per dollar of effort, this is the blanket to start with. Frost King lists the SP57/11C as a vinyl-backed fiberglass blanket, 3 inches thick and 48 by 75 inches, rated R10, and designed for gas, oil or electric water heaters up to 60 gallons. A roll of plastic tape is included to close the seam and patch the cut-outs. The one spec that matters is that R10 comes from 3 inches of real fiberglass, not from a thin reflective layer. That is why it adds a meaningful layer to an older tank with weak factory insulation: the rating does not depend on leaving a precise air gap around the tank, which is how most of the foil jackets reach their numbers. The trade-off is handling. Fiberglass itches and sheds when you cut it, so wear gloves, long sleeves and a dust mask, and tape every cut edge. The thickness also eats space, which matters in a closet where the tank sits close to the walls. On a gas heater you must trim it to keep the top, the burner access panel and the air openings at the base clear. Buy the larger Frost King SP60 instead for a tall or wide tank, or the SmartJacket if fiberglass is a deal-breaker."
  },
  {
    "id": "smartjacket-sj-80",
    "name": "SmartJacket SJ-80 5mm Polyethylene Water Heater Blanket",
    "category": "Polyethylene Foam, R-7.1 (Listed), 5 mm, Fits 20-80 Gal, No Fiberglass",
    "badge": "Best Fiberglass-Free",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "No fiberglass, listed as non-toxic and non-carcinogenic",
      "Fits 20 to 80 gallon tanks, gas or electric, per the listing",
      "Listed as Energy Star certified",
      "Thin 5 mm profile fits tanks that sit close to walls",
      "Smooth surface is easy to wipe clean"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "R-7.1 is a maker claim that relies on the jacket's air-gap design",
      "Lower rating than 3-inch fiberglass blankets",
      "Listing copy is heavy on marketing and light on install detail"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best water heater blanket if you want no fiberglass and a thin wrap that fits a crowded utility closet.",
    "description": "The SmartJacket SJ-80 is for anyone who wants a water heater blanket without handling fiberglass, or who has a tank squeezed into a closet where a 3-inch blanket will not fit. The listing describes a 5 mm polyethylene jacket that fits 20 to 80 gallon tanks, gas or electric, lists it as Energy Star certified, and says it contains no fiberglass and is non-toxic and non-carcinogenic. The number to understand is the R-7.1 rating. SmartJacket's own listing explains that the system creates a space between the blanket and the tank, and that trapped air layer is a large part of how a 5 mm jacket can claim a rating above R7. In practice, wrap it neatly and avoid crushing it flat against the steel, or you lose some of that gap. Owners commonly report that it is quick to install and cleaner to live with than fiberglass, especially in a laundry room. The trade-off is simple: on paper it insulates less than the Frost King's R10 fiberglass, and the rating depends on the installation. It is still a sensible choice for a tank that is only mildly warm, or where you value a clean, thin wrap. Buy the Frost King SP57/11C instead if the tank is hot to touch and you have room for the thickness."
  },
  {
    "id": "aes-industries-r8",
    "name": "AES Industries Water Heater Blanket Jacket Insulation R8",
    "category": "Non-Fiberglass Reflective Jacket, R8.9 (Listed), 6 mm, Fits 20-100 Gal",
    "badge": "Best for Big Tanks",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Listed to fit 20 to 100 gallon tanks, the widest range here",
      "Non-fiberglass, non-toxic and non-carcinogenic, per the listing",
      "6 mm thickness with an R8.9 value claimed in the listing",
      "Fits gas or electric water heaters",
      "Easy to install and easy to clean, per the listing"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Like other thin jackets, the rating depends on the air gap the system creates",
      "Listing claims of up to 40% heat-loss reduction are maker figures",
      "Still less insulation than a 3-inch fiberglass blanket"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The fiberglass-free jacket to buy for a large 75 or 80 gallon tank that standard blankets will not wrap.",
    "description": "Large tanks are where standard blankets run out of material, and the AES Industries jacket is the pick for anyone with a 75 or 80 gallon heater. Its listing says it fits all 20 to 100 gallon tanks, gas or electric, and describes a 6 mm non-fiberglass jacket with an R8.9 value, with no fiberglass and nothing toxic or carcinogenic in it. The spec that matters is coverage. A 48-inch blanket cannot close around a wide 80-gallon tank with any overlap, and a gap down one side leaks heat all day. A jacket sized for big tanks avoids that problem. Like the SmartJacket, AES says its system creates a space between the blanket and the tank, and that air layer is part of how a thin product reaches its stated rating, so wrap it snugly without crushing it. The trade-off is that a claimed R8.9 from 6 mm is not directly comparable with 3 inches of fiberglass; treat reflective-jacket numbers as best-case figures. The listing's claim of up to 40 percent less heat loss is a maker figure and the real result depends on how poorly insulated your tank is to begin with. Buy the Trade Winds R12 kit instead if you want the highest stated rating in a non-fiberglass wrap."
  },
  {
    "id": "trade-winds-r12-foam",
    "name": "Trade Winds Water Heater Blanket Jacket Foam Insulation R12",
    "category": "Reflective Foam Core, Foil Faced, R12 (Listed), 40-80 Gal, Tape Included",
    "badge": "Highest Listed R-Value",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Highest listed rating here at R12",
      "Reflective foam core with aluminum foil facing, no fiberglass",
      "Two pieces, 4 x 7 ft and 2 x 7 ft, cover tall 40 to 80 gallon tanks",
      "30 feet of reflective tape included for seams",
      "Separate pieces make cutting around panels easier"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Foil-faced foam ratings are maker figures that assume a proper install",
      "Two-piece kit means more seams to tape",
      "Too much material for a small 30-gallon tank"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The non-fiberglass kit to choose when you want the biggest stated R-value on a 40 to 80 gallon tank.",
    "description": "The Trade Winds R12 kit suits owners of a mid-size or large tank who want the highest rating they can get without fiberglass. The listing describes a reflective foam core with aluminum foil facing, supplied as two pieces, one 4 by 7 feet and one 2 by 7 feet, with 30 feet of reflective tape, and rates it R12 for 40 to 80 gallon tanks. The spec that matters is the stated R12, which on paper beats both the Frost King fiberglass blanket and the thin polyethylene jackets. Be realistic about what that number means. Ratings on foil-faced foam depend on the foil facing an air space and on a tight, well-taped install, so the figure is a best case for a careful job rather than a guarantee on any tank. The two-piece format is genuinely useful: the 7-foot height covers tall tanks top to bottom, and working with two narrower panels makes it easier to cut clean openings for the thermostat panels on an electric heater or to stop short of the burner area on a gas one. The trade-off is extra seams, and every seam needs taping or it leaks heat. It is also more material than a small tank needs. Buy the Frost King SP57/11C instead if you prefer a single-piece blanket with a well-understood fiberglass rating."
  },
  {
    "id": "frost-king-sp60",
    "name": "Frost King SP60 All Season Water Heater Insulation Blanket",
    "category": "Fiberglass Blanket, R10, 3 in Thick, 60 x 90 in",
    "badge": "Best for Tall Tanks",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "R10 fiberglass in a larger 60 by 90 inch size",
      "Extra width wraps wider tanks with room to overlap the seam",
      "Extra length covers tall tanks from base to top",
      "Same 3-inch thickness as the SP57/11C",
      "From Frost King, a long-standing weatherization brand"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Fiberglass needs protective gear when cutting",
      "Listing gives little detail beyond size and weight",
      "Overkill for a small 30 or 40 gallon tank"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The fiberglass blanket to buy when the standard 48 by 75 inch size will not wrap your tank with a proper overlap.",
    "description": "The SP60 is the bigger sibling of our top pick, and it exists for one reason: some tanks are simply too wide or too tall for a 48 by 75 inch blanket. Frost King lists it as an All Season blanket, 3 inches thick and 60 by 90 inches, rated R10, weighing 7 pounds. Measure before you choose. Wrap a tape measure around your tank and add a few inches for overlap; if that number is more than 48 inches, the SP57/11C will leave a gap, and a gap running the height of the tank undoes much of the point of insulating it. The extra 15 inches of length also helps on tall models where the standard blanket stops short. The insulation itself is the same as the smaller blanket, 3 inches of fiberglass rated R10, so it behaves predictably and does not depend on a carefully preserved air gap. The trade-offs are the usual fiberglass ones: wear gloves and a mask to cut it, tape every raw edge, and expect a thick, bulky wrap that needs clearance from walls. The listing itself is thin on detail, so rely on the dimensions. On a gas heater, trim it to keep the top, burner panel and air openings clear. Buy the AES jacket instead for a very large tank if you want to avoid fiberglass."
  },
  {
    "id": "trade-winds-r8-80-gal",
    "name": "Trade Winds Reflective Water Heater Jacket Insulation R8, 80 Gallon (Foil/Black)",
    "category": "Reflective Jacket, R8 (Listed), Fits 80 Gal, Class 1/Class A Fire Rated",
    "badge": "Best Fire-Rated Jacket",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Listed as Class 1 / Class A fire rated",
      "Sized for 80 gallon tanks",
      "Non-toxic and will not irritate skin, eyes or throat, per the listing",
      "Foil and black facing options",
      "Easy to clean surface"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "R8 is a maker figure for a reflective product",
      "Listing efficiency and savings claims are marketing figures",
      "Sized for large tanks, so it is a lot of material on a small one"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A large-tank reflective jacket with a stated fire rating, for anyone who wants that reassurance in a garage or utility room.",
    "description": "This Trade Winds jacket is aimed at owners of a big 80 gallon heater who want a reflective, fiberglass-free wrap with a stated fire rating. The listing rates it R8, sizes it for 80 gallon tanks, describes it as Class 1 / Class A fire rated and non-toxic, and says it will not irritate skin, eyes or throat. It is offered in foil and black facings. The spec that sets it apart is the fire rating. Any blanket near a water heater sits close to heat, and on a gas unit close to a burner and flue, so a stated Class A or Class 1 rating is a reasonable thing to look for, especially in a garage or a cramped utility room. It does not change the installation rules: keep the top, the burner compartment, the combustion air openings and the flue of a gas heater completely uncovered regardless of what the jacket is rated. The R8 rating is the same kind of maker figure as other reflective jackets, and the listing's claims of reduced heating bills and heat-loss cuts are marketing numbers whose real-world effect depends on your tank. The trade-off against the AES jacket is mainly range: AES claims to fit 20 to 100 gallons, while this one is sized for 80. Buy the Frost King SP60 instead if you prefer fiberglass on a large tank."
  },
  {
    "id": "ks-novelties-30-gal-r6",
    "name": "K's Novelties Reflective Water Heater Jacket Insulation, 30 Gallon, R6",
    "category": "Reflective Jacket, R6 (Listed), Fits 30 Gal Tanks, Tape Included, Made in USA",
    "badge": "Best for Small Tanks",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Sized specifically for 30 gallon tanks",
      "Tape included in the kit",
      "Made in USA, per the listing",
      "Thin reflective wrap suits tight closets and mobile homes",
      "No fiberglass to handle"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "R6 is the lowest listed rating here",
      "Very sparse listing detail",
      "Will not fit tanks larger than 30 gallons"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A right-sized reflective jacket for small 30 gallon tanks where a big blanket would mean cutting away half the material.",
    "description": "Small tanks are an awkward fit for most blankets, which are cut for 40 to 60 gallon heaters and leave you trimming away a large share of the material. This K's Novelties jacket is sized for 30 gallon tanks, rated R6, includes tape, and is listed as made in the USA. It suits a small electric heater in an apartment closet, a cabin, a mobile home or a single-person household where the tank is compact and the space around it is tight. The spec that matters here is fit. A jacket made for the tank's size means fewer seams, less waste and a cleaner job around the access panels, which matters because a poorly fitted blanket with gaps and loose edges does less than a modest one installed neatly. Be clear-eyed about the R6 rating: it is the lowest on this list and, like every reflective jacket, it is a maker figure that assumes a good installation. On a small tank in a heated space, the absolute energy saving is also smaller, so check with the touch test first that the tank is actually losing heat. The listing itself is very sparse, so confirm your tank's gallon size on the rating label before ordering. Buy the SmartJacket SJ-80 instead if you want a higher listed rating and do not mind trimming excess."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Water Heater Blankets",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-water-heater-blanket/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best water heater blanket?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most older 30 to 50 gallon tanks, the Frost King SP57/11C is the best water heater blanket: 3 inches of vinyl-backed fiberglass rated R10, sized for tanks up to 60 gallons, with tape included. If you want no fiberglass, the SmartJacket SJ-80 is the tidy alternative, and the AES Industries jacket suits very large tanks up to 100 gallons."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are water heater blankets worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They are worth it on tanks with insulation below R-24, which usually means older tanks that feel warm to the touch. The Department of Energy estimates such a blanket can cut standby heat loss by about 25 to 45 percent and save roughly 7 to 16 percent of water heating costs. On a modern, cool-to-touch tank the gain is small."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you put a blanket on a gas water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, as long as you keep the combustion parts clear. Do not cover the top, where the draft hood and flue sit, and stop the blanket well above the burner access panel and the air openings at the base. Keep all insulation away from the vent pipe, and follow the heater's manual, which may give model-specific rules."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should a water heater blanket cover the top?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On an electric water heater, many blanket instructions allow covering the top, leaving the pipe connections and relief valve exposed. On a gas water heater, never cover the top, because the draft hood and flue are there and the top runs hot from the exhaust. When the blanket and heater instructions disagree, follow the heater's manual."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do new water heaters need a blanket?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not. Tanks built to current federal efficiency standards are generally better insulated than older ones, and many already exceed R-24. Check the label or manual for the R-value and do the touch test. Some manufacturers state that a blanket is unnecessary for their models, and heat pump water heaters should not be wrapped at all."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does a water heater blanket void the warranty?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not automatically, but it can complicate a claim if it was installed badly. Manufacturers commonly warn against covering controls, labels, the relief valve or combustion air openings. Read the manual for your model, follow its conditions on external insulation, keep the rating plate visible, and keep a photo of the finished install with your paperwork."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is fiberglass or foil better for a water heater blanket?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Fiberglass blankets around 3 inches thick give a dependable R10 that does not depend on an air gap, so they suit tanks that are clearly losing heat. Foil-faced foam and polyethylene jackets are thinner, cleaner and fiberglass-free, but their stated ratings rely on careful installation. Choose fiberglass for maximum gain and foil where space or handling matters."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Appliances",
    tags: [
      "water heater blanket",
      "water heater insulation blanket",
      "water heater jacket",
      "hot water heater blanket",
      "water heater insulation"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Water Heater Blankets</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          An older tank that is warm to the touch is leaking money all day. Seven real blankets and jackets on Amazon, plus the gas and electric rules for wrapping one safely.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#frost-king-sp57-11c"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Shaheer Ali Khan</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Consumer Product Testing &amp; Materials Performance Specialist</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="3.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#do-you-need-one" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Do You Need One?</a>
          <a href="#r-value" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. R-Value Explained</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The 7 Best Water Heater Blankets</a>
          <a href="#gas-rules" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Gas Heater Rules</a>
          <a href="#electric-rules" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Electric Heater Rules</a>
          <a href="#how-to-install" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. How to Install One</a>
          <a href="#savings" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. How Much It Saves</a>
          <a href="#warranty" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Warranty Caveats</a>
          <a href="#alternatives" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. When a Blanket Is the Wrong Fix</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A water heater blanket is one of the cheapest efficiency fixes in the house, but only on the right tank. This guide explains which tanks benefit, what R-value really means for fiberglass and foil jackets, and the clearances gas and electric heaters need, then ranks seven real blankets.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="do-you-need-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Do You Need a Water Heater Blanket? Only If the Tank Is Below R-24
          </h2>

          <p>
            A water heater blanket is worth adding only to a tank that is losing heat through its own walls. The U.S. Department of Energy's guidance is that a tank with insulation below R-24 is a candidate, and that insulating it can cut standby heat loss by roughly 25 to 45 percent, worth around 7 to 16 percent of water heating costs. A modern, well-insulated tank gains very little. Three quick checks tell you which you have.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Warm-to-Touch Test</h3>

          <p>
            Put your palm flat on the side of the tank, away from the pipes and the burner area. If the shell feels warm, heat is escaping through the walls into the room, and a blanket will slow it. If it feels at or near room temperature, the factory insulation is already doing its job and a blanket will make little measurable difference. This is the same test the Department of Energy suggests, and it takes ten seconds.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Label for an R-Value</h3>

          <p>
            Many tanks print an insulation R-value on the rating label or in the manual. Anything under R-24 is worth wrapping. Tanks that already carry more than that, or that the manufacturer describes as high-efficiency with thick foam insulation, rarely justify a blanket. If the label says nothing, the touch test and the tank's age are your next clues.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use the Tank's Age as a Shortcut</h3>

          <p>
            Older tanks generally have thinner factory insulation than current models, which were built to tighter federal efficiency standards. A tank more than about ten years old that is warm to the touch is the classic case for a blanket. If you are not sure how old yours is, our guide on <a href="/how-to-tell-the-age-of-a-water-heater/">how to tell the age of a water heater</a> decodes the serial number for the main brands.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Skip It</h3>

          <p>
            Skip the blanket on a tank that is already cool to the touch, on a heat pump water heater that needs free airflow around it, and on any unit whose manual tells you not to add one. If the tank is old, warm and also rumbling or rusting at the fittings, the money is usually better spent on a replacement such as one of our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">best gas water heaters</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="r-value" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. R-Value Explained: Fiberglass R10 vs Thin Foil Jackets
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water heater blankets come in two families, and their R-values are not measured the same way in practice. Knowing the difference stops you from buying a 5 mm jacket because its number looks close to a 3-inch blanket's.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fiberglass Blankets</h3>

          <p>
            Vinyl-backed fiberglass blankets, like the Frost King SP57/11C and SP60, are about 3 inches thick and rated R10. That rating comes from the thickness of the fiberglass itself, so it holds up as long as the blanket is not crushed. They are the most predictable option for an older tank that is clearly losing heat. The downside is handling: fiberglass needs gloves, sleeves and a mask when you cut it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Foil-Faced Foam and Polyethylene Jackets</h3>

          <p>
            Jackets like the SmartJacket, AES and Trade Winds are thin foam or polyethylene, often with a reflective foil face, and claim ratings from R6 to R12 in a few millimeters. They are cleaner to handle, contain no fiberglass, and fit closets where a thick blanket will not. They also wipe clean, which matters in a laundry room or garage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Thin-Jacket Ratings Need an Asterisk</h3>

          <p>
            Reflective products reach their stated numbers partly through the air layer next to the foil. Several listings say so directly, describing a system that creates a space between the blanket and the tank. That means the real result depends on installation: a jacket pulled tight and crushed flat against the steel, or full of untaped seams, will underperform its label. Treat these ratings as best-case figures.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Choose Between Them</h3>

          <p>
            If the tank is hot to the touch and you have room, fiberglass R10 gives the most dependable gain. If space is tight, if you cannot face fiberglass, or the tank is only mildly warm, a reflective jacket is a reasonable, tidier choice. Either way, size it to the tank so the seam overlaps instead of leaving a gap.
          </p>

