        <div id="gas-rules" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Gas Water Heater Blankets: Keep the Top, Burner and Flue Clear
          </h2>

          <p>
            A blanket on a gas water heater is safe only if it leaves the parts that handle combustion completely alone. A gas heater burns fuel under the tank and sends hot exhaust up a flue through its center, and any insulation that blocks air in or exhaust out creates a real hazard. These rules come before any efficiency gain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Leave the Top Uncovered</h3>

          <p>
            On a gas heater, do not wrap the top. The draft hood and flue connection sit there, and the top of the tank runs hot from the exhaust passing through the center flue. Stop the blanket at the shoulder of the tank, below the top edge, and never fold material over the draft hood or let it touch the vent pipe.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep the Burner Compartment and Air Openings Open</h3>

          <p>
            The burner and pilot sit behind an access panel at the base, and the burner draws combustion air through openings near the bottom of the tank. Cut the blanket short so it ends well above that area, and leave the burner access panel, the gas control valve and the air intake openings completely clear. A blanket that starves the burner of air can cause incomplete combustion.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Not Touch the Flue or Vent Pipe</h3>

          <p>
            The vent pipe carries hot combustion gases, including carbon monoxide, out of the house. Keep every part of the blanket and its tape away from it. If your heater shares a small closet with a furnace, the clearances get tight quickly, so measure before you buy a thick fiberglass blanket.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Power Vent and Sealed Combustion Units</h3>

          <p>
            Power vent and direct vent gas heaters are generally newer and better insulated, and their manuals often include specific guidance on external blankets. Read it before wrapping one. Many such units are already well above R-24, so the touch test often tells you a blanket is not needed. Our <a href="/best-power-vent-water-heater/">power vent water heater guide</a> covers how those units differ.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="electric-rules" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Electric Water Heater Blankets: Never Cover the Thermostat Panels
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric water heaters are simpler to wrap because there is no flame or flue, but they still have parts that must stay accessible. The main rule is to cut openings for the thermostat and heating element access panels instead of covering them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cut Out the Upper and Lower Access Panels</h3>

          <p>
            Most electric tanks have two removable covers on the side, one near the top and one near the bottom, hiding the thermostats and heating elements. Mark their outline on the blanket and cut a flap or opening so you can reach them without removing the blanket. Covering them traps heat around the controls and makes future repairs, such as replacing a <a href="/water-heater-element/">water heater element</a>, harder.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Covering the Top Is Usually Fine</h3>

          <p>
            Unlike a gas heater, an electric tank has no flue, so many blanket instructions allow you to cover the top. Leave the pipe connections, the temperature and pressure relief valve and its discharge pipe uncovered, and follow both the blanket's and the heater's instructions.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep the Rating Label Visible</h3>

          <p>
            The rating plate shows the model, serial number, wattage and safety warnings. Leave it exposed, or cut a window around it. A technician, an inspector or a warranty claim will need it, and covering safety labels is something manufacturers commonly warn against.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Set the Thermostat Sensibly</h3>

          <p>
            A blanket keeps the water hot longer, which makes a high thermostat setting less necessary. The Department of Energy suggests 120 degrees Fahrenheit for most homes, which saves energy and reduces scalding risk. If you are replacing an electric tank rather than insulating it, our <a href="/best-heat-pump-water-heater/">heat pump water heater guide</a> covers the far more efficient option.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How to Install a Water Heater Blanket in About 30 Minutes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Installing a water heater blanket is a short, low-skill job. The care goes into the cut-outs, not the wrapping. Turn off power to an electric heater, or set a gas heater to pilot, before you start working around the controls.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Measure the Tank</h3>

          <p>
            Measure the circumference and the height. Add a few inches to the circumference for an overlap at the seam. Check this against the blanket's dimensions before you open it, since a blanket that will not close leaves a gap the full height of the tank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Mark Every Opening</h3>

          <p>
            Hold the blanket in place and mark the access panels, the rating label, the drain valve, the temperature and pressure relief valve, and on a gas heater the line where the blanket must stop above the burner area. Mark generously; it is easier to tape a slightly larger opening than to fix one that is too small.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Cut and Wrap</h3>

          <p>
            Cut the openings with a utility knife, wearing gloves and a mask for fiberglass. Wrap the blanket around the tank with the seam overlapping, ideally on a side where you can reach it, and hold it with the supplied tape at the top, middle and bottom.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Tape Seams and Edges</h3>

          <p>
            Run tape along the full seam and around every cut edge. Taped edges stop fiberglass shedding and close the gaps that let heat escape. Then check the clearances again and restore power or turn the gas heater back on.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Insulate the First Few Feet of Pipe</h3>

          <p>
            The hot water outlet loses heat as well. Foam sleeves on the first several feet of the hot and cold lines finish the job; our <a href="/best-pipe-insulation/">best pipe insulation guide</a> covers the sizes and materials. On a gas heater, keep pipe insulation at least 6 inches from the flue.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="savings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Much Does a Water Heater Blanket Save? Most on an Old Tank in a Cold Room
          </h2>

          <p>
            The honest answer is that it depends on two things: how poorly insulated your tank is, and how cold the space around it is. The Department of Energy's estimate of around 7 to 16 percent of water heating costs applies to tanks below R-24, not to every tank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where the Tank Sits Matters</h3>

          <p>
            A tank in an unheated garage or a cold basement loses heat faster than one in a heated utility room, because the temperature difference across the tank wall is larger. Insulating a tank in the cold is where blankets pay back quickest, and it also matters more in winter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat Lost to a Heated Room Is Not All Wasted</h3>

          <p>
            In winter, some of the heat leaking from a tank inside your living space warms the house. That does not make the leak free, since water heating is often a pricier way to heat a room, but it means the real saving on a tank inside conditioned space is smaller than the headline percentage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Payback Is Usually Quick</h3>

          <p>
            A blanket is an inexpensive one-time purchase and the energy it saves recurs every day, so on an old, warm tank the payback is generally short. On a modern tank that already passes the touch test, the payback may never arrive, which is why the test comes first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Combine It With the Cheap Fixes</h3>

          <p>
            Lowering the thermostat to 120 degrees, insulating the first few feet of pipe, and fixing dripping hot taps each save money for little cost. Together with a blanket they get most of the savings available without replacing the heater.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Will a Blanket Void Your Water Heater Warranty? Read the Manual First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Adding an external blanket is a common, inexpensive upgrade, but water heater manufacturers do not all treat it the same way. Check before you wrap, especially on a tank still under warranty.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the Manual Usually Says</h3>

          <p>
            Manuals often address external insulation directly. Some say a blanket is unnecessary because the tank is already well insulated, some give conditions such as keeping the controls, the relief valve, the labels and the combustion areas clear, and some discourage blankets on particular models. The manual for your exact model is the authority, not a general rule.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Installs Cause Problems</h3>

          <p>
            Trouble comes from covering things that should stay exposed. A blanket that blocks combustion air on a gas unit, covers warning labels or the rating plate, or traps heat around electric controls can contribute to a fault, and that can complicate a warranty claim. A careful installation that follows both sets of instructions avoids those issues.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep Records</h3>

          <p>
            Keep the blanket's instructions and a photo of the finished install with the heater's paperwork. If a technician later needs to service the tank, they can see exactly what was done, and you can remove the blanket quickly if asked.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When in Doubt, Ask</h3>

          <p>
            If the manual is unclear, a short call to the manufacturer's support line or a question to the plumber who installed it settles it. It is a better use of five minutes than risking coverage on a tank that may have years of warranty left.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When a Blanket Is the Wrong Fix
          </h2>

          <p>
            A blanket solves one problem, heat leaking through the walls of an under-insulated tank. Several common water heater complaints have different causes, and wrapping the tank will not help them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Running Out of Hot Water</h3>

          <p>
            A blanket keeps stored water hot longer between uses but does not add capacity or speed recovery. If the last shower runs cold, the tank is undersized or its burner or elements are struggling. Our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">gas water heater guide</a> explains first hour rating, the number that predicts whether a tank keeps up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">An Old, Noisy or Leaking Tank</h3>

          <p>
            If the tank is past ten years, rumbling, or showing rust at the fittings, insulating it only delays a replacement. See <a href="/how-long-do-water-heaters-last/">how long water heaters last</a> for the warning signs. A new tank will be better insulated from the factory.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">High Electric Bills From an Electric Tank</h3>

          <p>
            A blanket trims standby loss, but the bigger gain for an electric household usually comes from replacing the tank with a heat pump water heater, which uses far less electricity to heat the same water. Those units need airflow, so do not wrap them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sediment and Slow Heating</h3>

          <p>
            Sediment on the bottom of a tank insulates the water from the burner and wastes energy in a way no blanket can fix. Flushing the tank, covered in <a href="/how-to-drain-a-water-heater/">how to drain a water heater</a>, restores performance. While you are at the tank, check the <a href="/best-water-heater-anode-rod/">water heater anode rod</a>, the part that decides how long the tank lasts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Wrapping a Tank That Is Already Well Insulated</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Modern tanks often carry R-24 or more from the factory. If the shell is cool to the touch and the label shows a high R-value, a blanket adds little but cost and clutter. Do the ten-second touch test and check the label before buying anything, and save the money for pipe insulation or a lower thermostat setting instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Covering the Top and Burner of a Gas Heater</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The top of a gas tank carries the draft hood and flue, and the base holds the burner and combustion air openings. Covering either can block air or exhaust and create a carbon monoxide risk. Stop the blanket below the top edge and well above the burner panel, and keep all material away from the vent pipe.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taping Over the Thermostat Panels and Labels</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On an electric heater the access panels hide the thermostats and elements, and the rating plate carries the model and safety warnings. Covering them makes repairs harder and can complicate warranty service. Cut neat openings around every panel, valve and label, and tape the cut edges instead of the panels.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Blanket That Will Not Close Around the Tank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A blanket that leaves a gap down one side lets heat escape along the whole height of the tank. Measure the circumference and add a few inches for overlap before you order, and choose a larger blanket or a jacket sized for big tanks if the standard 48 inch width falls short.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Crushing a Reflective Jacket Flat Against the Tank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thin foil and foam jackets rely partly on a layer of trapped air to reach their stated ratings. Pulling them drum-tight, leaving seams untaped or squashing them against walls loses part of that benefit. Wrap snugly but not crushed, tape every seam, and keep the jacket off nearby walls where you can.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best water heater blanket?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most older 30 to 50 gallon tanks, the Frost King SP57/11C is the best water heater blanket: 3 inches of vinyl-backed fiberglass rated R10, sized for tanks up to 60 gallons, with tape included. If you want no fiberglass, the SmartJacket SJ-80 is the tidy alternative, and the AES Industries jacket suits very large tanks up to 100 gallons.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are water heater blankets worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are worth it on tanks with insulation below R-24, which usually means older tanks that feel warm to the touch. The Department of Energy estimates such a blanket can cut standby heat loss by about 25 to 45 percent and save roughly 7 to 16 percent of water heating costs. On a modern, cool-to-touch tank the gain is small.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put a blanket on a gas water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, as long as you keep the combustion parts clear. Do not cover the top, where the draft hood and flue sit, and stop the blanket well above the burner access panel and the air openings at the base. Keep all insulation away from the vent pipe, and follow the heater's manual, which may give model-specific rules.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should a water heater blanket cover the top?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On an electric water heater, many blanket instructions allow covering the top, leaving the pipe connections and relief valve exposed. On a gas water heater, never cover the top, because the draft hood and flue are there and the top runs hot from the exhaust. When the blanket and heater instructions disagree, follow the heater's manual.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do new water heaters need a blanket?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Tanks built to current federal efficiency standards are generally better insulated than older ones, and many already exceed R-24. Check the label or manual for the R-value and do the touch test. Some manufacturers state that a blanket is unnecessary for their models, and heat pump water heaters should not be wrapped at all.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a water heater blanket void the warranty?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not automatically, but it can complicate a claim if it was installed badly. Manufacturers commonly warn against covering controls, labels, the relief valve or combustion air openings. Read the manual for your model, follow its conditions on external insulation, keep the rating plate visible, and keep a photo of the finished install with your paperwork.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is fiberglass or foil better for a water heater blanket?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fiberglass blankets around 3 inches thick give a dependable R10 that does not depend on an air gap, so they suit tanks that are clearly losing heat. Foil-faced foam and polyethylene jackets are thinner, cleaner and fiberglass-free, but their stated ratings rely on careful installation. Choose fiberglass for maximum gain and foil where space or handling matters.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

